WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
Why are small businesses obsessed with AI slop?
You’ve definitely seen these around.&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.280
Small businesses everywhere are plastering their&nbsp;
windows, menus, social media, notice boards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.560
and pretty much everything else, with AI.
And most of it looks awful.&nbsp;

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.320
Honestly, I’m starting to&nbsp;
miss the generic Canva ones.&nbsp;

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.000
How did small businesses&nbsp;
become so obsessed with AI?&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.920
You’ve very likely seen these around. If&nbsp;
you live in a city, just walk downtown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:41.840
and you’ll see a plethora of posters&nbsp;
with uncanny, unnatural-looking food.&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:48.600
This is just, awful. It doesn’t look&nbsp;
like food, it looks like… something else.&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.080
It’s not just street signs. There’s window&nbsp;
posters, mailbox flyers, they’ll be on bulletin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.560
boards, you’ll see them as event posters in bars&nbsp;
and community centers, market stalls, loyalty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.520
cards, club fundraisers, local sports teams.
Have you noticed a lot of it looks the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.320
in very specific ways?
It’s got big bold text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.680
on a slight angle on the left side, typically&nbsp;
two words, one in color. A huge amount of text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.920
typically a lot of meaningless icons, and&nbsp;
an oversaturated, somewhat uncanny AI image.&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.000
And these posters are everywhere.
After Intuit surveyed over 2,200 U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.800
small businesses, two-thirds admitted they used&nbsp;
AI regularly, while 28% of them used it daily.&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.480
Even 53% of those not using AI were&nbsp;
considering adopting it in the future.&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.800
And the U.S. Chamber of Commerce also noticed a&nbsp;
rise in generative AI use among business owners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:50.200
from 23% in 2023 to 40% in 2024 and 58% in 2025.
While these self-reported surveys roll out big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.520
numbers, researchers looking&nbsp;
at something more concrete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.040
like whether businesses were actually paying&nbsp;
for AI services, found a strange disconnect.&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:06.680
JPMorganChase found that 17.7% of small firms had&nbsp;
ever paid for an AI service by the end of 2025.&nbsp;

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.480
So, many businesses were using AI, but&nbsp;
most seemed to be using free functions.&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.160
But what are small businesses&nbsp;
actually doing with it in practice?&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:22.400
A 2026 report from the National Restaurant&nbsp;
Association found that 26% of restaurant operators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.600
are already using AI for everything from marketing&nbsp;
and inventory management to employee scheduling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:33.800
menu optimization, and taking orders.
So hence the oversaturation with AI image slop.&nbsp;

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.160
It’s not even images either.
Some are now using AI-generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:42.080
music in store, to cut down on costs.
And there’s a reason marketing keeps coming up.&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.720
The 2025 Federal Reserve Small Business&nbsp;
Credit Survey found that 46% of employer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.160
firms were using AI in some capacity.
But when you look at what they were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:56.520
using it for, 83% of those businesses&nbsp;
were using AI for writing or marketing.&nbsp;

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.840
That was much higher than using&nbsp;
it for individual productivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:07.800
at 61%, or planning and analysis, at 51%.
So yes, AI adoption is rising. But using an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:14.280
AI tool doesn’t necessarily mean a business has a&nbsp;
mature AI system or a clear strategy for using it.&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:20.320
Take Lisa, a freelance graphic designer in Spain.&nbsp;
After ChatGPT launched in 2022, she noticed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.280
her usual logo and packaging jobs started&nbsp;
disappearing, only to be replaced by clients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.800
asking her to clean up AI-generated designs.
By 2025, Lisa said 90% of her incoming logo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.640
and packaging requests involved&nbsp;
fixing AI-generated content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:42.120
and this made up 60% to 70% of her annual income.
Ironically, after skipping designers for AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.560
clients came back still expecting them to fix&nbsp;
the mess quickly and cheaply, even when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.360
designs had to be rebuilt from scratch.
Now, there isn’t anything inherently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.200
bad about all these AI designs.
A small business isn’t committing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.560
a crime or fraud, aside from maybe a bit of&nbsp;
false advertising with the images of food.&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.840
And those making these aren’t bad people.
In fact, the most common case does seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.560
be small businesses, local communities,&nbsp;
clubs, as opposed to big business.&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.440
But, as we’re about to see, the&nbsp;
consequences can be pretty devastating.&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.360
So, why do this?
How did we get here?&nbsp;

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.120
This video isn’t to hate on Small Businesses.&nbsp;
I’m actually passionate about small business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.200
owners doing well. They’re the backbone of&nbsp;
their communities and the United States.&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.360
And we’re in the best time in&nbsp;
history to start a business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.400
and you don’t need to start a restaurant, pay a&nbsp;
huge amount for real estate, staff, and inventory.&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.080
You don’t need a business that forces&nbsp;
you to work 7 days and long hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

