00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.480
In San Francisco, Grind &amp; Unwind&nbsp;
replaced a local favorite restaurant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.040
and soon put up AI-generated menus.
And yeah… this looks horrendous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:09.440
Doesn’t make me want to eat here.
It didn’t take long for people to notice.&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.760
The menus eventually made their way&nbsp;
to Reddit, where they were mocked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.360
with many comparing the focaccia in&nbsp;
the images to a loofah or even a lung.&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.640
Someone even graffitied the storefront.
Within the week, the owners removed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.480
display, and estimated it cost&nbsp;
$700 to cover up the graffiti.&nbsp;

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:31.320
But this was meant to be a temporary fix while&nbsp;
the owners, AJ and Lyndsey Lozano, figured out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.840
how to run a restaurant for the first time, all&nbsp;
while taking care of three children under five.&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.520
“We kind of jumped on the opportunity when the&nbsp;
space became available,” Lozano told SFGATE.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.320
“It was a very, very fast turnaround, so people&nbsp;
were like, ‘What happened to the Citrus Club?’”&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.440
“Honestly, we just kinda wanna move forward and&nbsp;
forget about the sign and hope people come try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.120
our quality food and really make it a cool&nbsp;
spot that locals can gather,” Lozano said.&nbsp;

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.960
I think this gives us a better&nbsp;
idea of what’s really happening.&nbsp;

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.120
Marketing is one of the&nbsp;
weaknesses of small businesses.&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.720
The University of Phoenix found that&nbsp;
marketing and advertising was the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.680
common skill owners said they lacked.
Business owners also tend to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.040
marketing tasks… because they lack&nbsp;
the knowledge of how to tackle them.&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.680
People often start a business because&nbsp;
they’re good at a skill or trade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.440
but don’t know how to market it.
And there’s more to it.&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:27.720
In 2024, the median full-service restaurant&nbsp;
made just 2.8% of its sales in pre-tax income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:34.240
For limited-service restaurants, it was about&nbsp;
4%, while labor alone ate up 36.5% of sales.&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:40.120
So, there isn’t a ton of money, or time, for a&nbsp;
robust, perfectly designed marketing strategy.&nbsp;

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.720
In 2024, 32% of 1,385 small-business operators&nbsp;
said they struggled with marketing because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:52.480
didn’t have enough time, budget, or skills.
And the most time-consuming marketing task?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.640
Creating content.
There’s also a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.520
pressure today to build a social media presence,&nbsp;
or even turn your business into content creation.&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.160
It has to look right, be professional,&nbsp;
but also not be too professional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.760
and you have to post a lot.
And AI offers an easy way out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.760
when you are running a restaurant on a&nbsp;
3% margin, while taking care of kids.&nbsp;

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:17.480
And, if we look closer at AI, it makes&nbsp;
sense why it seems like the solution.&nbsp;

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:22.320
Adoption has become frictionless. It’s now&nbsp;
so easy to instantly generate a graphic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.480
with all the text and imagery you want.
This is the same reason small businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.120
used to… and still do, use lots of&nbsp;
mismatched clip art, strange fonts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:38.000
and Comic Sans. Most just aren’t designers.
So, at a glance, AI might look professional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.280
Even if it’s not.
But that’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:45.680
the biggest problem, as we’ll see later.
Regardless, I don’t think business owners think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.080
“I got out of paying a designer!”
Maybe a few do, but most probably think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.040
“Great, one less thing on my to-do&nbsp;
list.” “The Facebook page finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:59.200
has content.” “I finally have a poster for&nbsp;
the window.” “The menu is finally done.”&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.840
And with the economy already putting&nbsp;
pressure on small businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:08.320
AI is becoming harder to live without.
77% of small businesses that use AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:12.720
say that limits on the technology would hurt&nbsp;
their growth, operations, and bottom line.&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:18.720
A common saying is “Don’t be so busy working in&nbsp;
the business that you can’t work on the business.”&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.440
And I think all these AI posters and menus&nbsp;
prove that this is exactly what happens.&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.240
Am I telling you to go eat food at&nbsp;
a restaurant with an AI poster…?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:34.160
No. Over time, people will realize this doesn’t&nbsp;
really work. We vote with our wallets, after all.&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.560
But, also know, the people who made&nbsp;
it probably weren’t malicious or lazy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:43.560
The priorities just need adjusting.
But the consequences can be pretty severe.&nbsp;

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.800
If you’ve watched this far,&nbsp;
you probably like our stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.920
so consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:05.680
Firstly, we just have the data that AI ads don’t&nbsp;
really work. At least, the more obviously AI ones.&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.320
NielsenIQ tested AI-generated&nbsp;
advertisements on more than 2,000 people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.800
with around 150 participants also having&nbsp;
their brain activity measured using EEG.&nbsp;

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:21.080
People generally found the AI-generated&nbsp;
ads more annoying, boring, and confusing.&nbsp;

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:25.880
Even the higher-quality AI ads produced weaker&nbsp;
memory activation than conventional ads.&nbsp;

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:29.560
And when the visuals looked&nbsp;
cheap or obviously AI-generated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:34.760
they required even more effort for people to&nbsp;
process, distracting them from the actual message.&nbsp;

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:40.720
So AI, and especially the really bad AI slop,&nbsp;
can actually take more effort to process.&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.280
And these businesses are about to find out why&nbsp;
companies have entire marketing departments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:48.200
dedicated to things that&nbsp;
might seem trivial at first…&nbsp;

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:52.600
Like, what color is more likely to trigger&nbsp;
a conversion, like a click or purchase?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:56.160
What should the customer notice first?&nbsp;
How do we guide the customer’s eye?&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.680
Advertising research has found that the easier&nbsp;
an ad is to understand, both visually and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:05.400
semantically, the more positively people&nbsp;
tend to respond to the ad and the brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:09.160
and the more likely they are to consider buying.
This is why you can learn what a business is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:12.040
selling from just a poster,&nbsp;
even while you’re distracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:17.240
by a hundred other things, like driving.
But AI, ironically, is making that harder.&nbsp;

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:20.720
Instead of immediately understanding&nbsp;
the message, the viewer has to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.520
extra cognitive effort figuring out what&nbsp;
they’re looking at in the first place.&nbsp;

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:29.480
And this problem is huge in the food sector.
When you see an image of food, you’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:34.720
just judging whether the picture looks good.
Your brain is also asking… is this edible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:37.120
familiar, or safe?
Most people reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:42.160
a different conclusion… “uncanny.”
A 2025 study published in Appetite found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:47.800
that imperfect AI-generated food could trigger&nbsp;
an “uncanny valley” response, with participants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:53.160
finding these images more uncanny than both&nbsp;
realistic and deliberately unrealistic images.&nbsp;

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.680
The researchers also factored in two&nbsp;
things that could explain this reaction:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:02.080
food disgust and food neophobia, which is&nbsp;
basically an aversion to unfamiliar food.&nbsp;

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.880
And interestingly, food&nbsp;
neophobia drove up this effect.&nbsp;

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:09.880
For example, a cartoon taco&nbsp;
doesn’t need to look real.&nbsp;

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:14.920
But a nearly photorealistic AI taco with…&nbsp;
impossible textures, fused ingredients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:21.040
or sauce that seems to defy physics is confusing.
Even more so, people will assume the AI quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:24.720
is also a reflection of the&nbsp;
quality of the actual product.&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.440
Whether that’s true or not&nbsp;
doesn’t actually matter.&nbsp;

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.720
As a marketing professor once told me:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:33.400
In marketing, perception is&nbsp;
more important than reality.&nbsp;

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:38.600
Ironically, marketing and branding is the last&nbsp;
thing a lot of small business owners think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:43.560
since they’re so busy actually running the place.
But, it’s one of the most important things for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:48.960
customers, because it can decide whether they even&nbsp;
try the business, and become a returning customer.&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:54.000
But one of the biggest consequences is that&nbsp;
small businesses can sacrifice their advantage.&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.880
And this creates another problem.
Small businesses have something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:03.000
that huge corporations spend millions&nbsp;
trying to manufacture…personality.&nbsp;

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.520
You don’t go to a small family-run&nbsp;
Chinese restaurant because you expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.360
it to look like McDonald’s.
Even if the food doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:14.640
have the perfect photos on the menu.
Those imperfections are part of the identity.&nbsp;

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.800
It’s family run, and is full of personality&nbsp;
and culture. From the decor to the chairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:22.120
to the mismatched branding.
You see this all over Japan too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:25.680
Those hidden, tiny restaurants down&nbsp;
an alley, run by one grandparent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:30.480
that just have an “open” sign and nothing else.
But AI can start eroding that.&nbsp;

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:35.040
Because when every restaurant uses the same glossy&nbsp;
templates, perfect food photography, cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:40.160
lighting, and polished AI-generated graphics…
Eventually, a family-run restaurant around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:44.600
block starts looking like every other business.
A small business starts losing one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:49.080
biggest things that help it compete with&nbsp;
the giant chains in the first place.&nbsp;

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:55.080
There is a silver lining. Some businesses have&nbsp;
realized this, and are leaning the other way.&nbsp;

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:59.600
Leaning into a unique, non-AI&nbsp;
style. Embracing imperfect photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:04.400
graphics that look a bit unprofessional, but&nbsp;
ironically, there’s a good bit of charm to it!&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:09.920
This is called “strategic imperfection,”&nbsp;
and it was around before AI was a thing.&nbsp;

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:14.160
But people seem much more receptive&nbsp;
to those imperfect details now.&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:19.320
Of course, you can’t design all ads and&nbsp;
posters in MS Paint… but a surprising number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:24.480
of people would rather buy from a brand&nbsp;
with an MS Paint sign… than an AI sign.&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.520
One of the biggest players in AI, OpenAI, is&nbsp;
actually in a difficult spot. They’ve made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.960
about a trillion dollar in purchase commitments,&nbsp;
but, it’s not turning out the way they expected.&nbsp;

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:39.680
It seems OpenAI might own the least&nbsp;
valuable part of the AI ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:48.081
The AI landscape seems to be changing.&nbsp;
